\description{
One row per parameter and level, with these recovery metrics: bias,
RMSE, the coverage and mean width of the \code{ci_level} interval and of the
central 50 \% interval, the Pearson correlation with a
Fisher-z interval, the Spearman correlation, and Lin's concordance
with its interval, its decomposition and a calibration line, slope and
intercept (see \code{\link[=recovery_ccc]{recovery_ccc()}} for how to read them).
}
\details{
Correlation metrics are \code{NA}, never \code{0}, when fewer than three
complete pairs are available or when either side has no spread: \code{0}
would read as "no recovery" where the honest answer is "not estimable
from this design". The correlation interval is a 95\% confidence
interval and does not follow \code{ci_level}, which is the mass of the
posterior interval and a different quantity.

Population and SD rows are summarised across replications, one pair
per replication. Subject-level objects are summarised \strong{within
replication and then combined}. Pooling subjects across replications
would mix between-subject with between-replication variance. \code{r} and \code{rank_r}
are combined on Fisher's z scale with weights \code{n - 3}; \code{ccc} on Lin's
Z scale with weights from his asymptotic variance, and without an
interval if any replication has no variance (three subjects, or a
coefficient of exactly 0 or 1); \code{ccc_scale_shift} and
\code{calibration_slope} as geometric means; \code{ccc_accuracy},
\code{ccc_location_shift} and \code{calibration_intercept} as means (an
intercept can be 0 or negative); \code{truth_sd} as the root mean variance.
\code{calibration_intercept} is read on the scale of the row: the same
fits scored on the link and on the natural scale give two different
intercepts, each the value of its own calibration line at 0.
At this level \code{ccc = r * ccc_accuracy} holds only approximately.

Rows are grouped by \code{estimator} as well as by term and level, so two
estimators of the same parameter scored against one truth give two
rows rather than one pooled bias and RMSE. Their \code{n} may differ when
one of them failed on a subject the other estimated; \code{\link[=recover]{recover()}} warns
when it does.

\code{detected} is the share of replications whose interval excludes zero
and \code{sign_recovery} the share whose estimate has the truth's sign. What
\code{detected} measures depends on the truth beside it: a false-positive
rate at a true value of zero, power at a true effect, and on a
parameter whose true effect is zero while another carries one, the rate
at which an effect leaks into a parameter that has none.
\code{sign_recovery} is \code{NA} when the truth is zero, which has no sign. Both
are \code{NA} at the subject level, where a row summarises a correlation
across people rather than one estimate against one truth.

\strong{Two coverages.} \code{coverage} is the share of \code{ci_level} intervals
(95 \% by default) that contain the generating value and \code{coverage_50}
the share of central 50 \% intervals that do. A calibrated posterior has
both at their nominal mass; the 95 \% interval checks the tails and the
50 \% interval the centre, so a posterior that is too narrow near its
median and too wide in its tails can be nominal on one and not on the
other. \code{coverage_50} is \code{NA} when the estimates carry no 50 \% interval,
as with a hand-built tibble that has no \code{ci_low_50}/\code{ci_high_50}.

\strong{The print.} Printing the summary shows a core set of columns ---
the condition and, on a grid, the design columns after it, the term,
the estimator when there is more than one,
the level, the scale, \code{n}, \code{bias}, \code{rmse}, \code{coverage}, \code{coverage_50},
\code{z_mean}, \code{z_sd}, \code{contraction}, \code{r}, \code{ccc}, \code{reliability_true} and
\code{reliability_model} when there are subject rows, the calibration
columns, and \code{precision_target} and \code{precision_met} when \code{precision}
was given --- and says how many it hides.
The object itself keeps all of them; \code{tibble::as_tibble()} prints
every one.

\code{n_converged} is the number of replications whose fit passed
\code{\link[=check_convergence]{check_convergence()}}, read from the \code{converged} column that
\code{\link[=extract_estimates]{extract_estimates()}} fills. It is \code{NA} when no fit carried a
verdict, as with a hand-built estimates tibble: reporting it as equal
to \code{n} there would assert something that was never measured.
